    "description": {
      "type": "doc",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "text": "The Highlands cover the north and west of Scotland, beyond the Highland Boundary Fault that runs from Arran to Stonehaven. Ben Nevis rises 1,345 metres above Fort William, and the Great Glen cuts a straight line from there to Inverness through Loch Ness.",
              "type": "text"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "text": "Clans held this land for centuries until the Jacobite defeat at Culloden in 1746. The Clearances that followed emptied whole glens for sheep, and ruined crofts still mark the hillsides. Gaelic survives on road signs and in the speech of the west coast and islands.",
              "type": "text"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "text": "May and September bring the driest spells and fewer midges than high summer. Carry a waterproof on every walk, since the weather on the tops can turn within the hour.",
              "type": "text"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
